    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 301921\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1221 PM MST Wed Sep 30 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Aside from a few showers possible in far eastern Gila County this\n  afternoon, dry conditions with near zero rain chances will \n  persist through the next several days.\n\n- As high pressure gradually builds over the region, a gradual\n  warming trend is expected with near to slightly above normal\n  temperatures returning by the end of the week and into the \n  weekend. \n  \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH FRIDAY/... \nLatest water vapor satellite imagery indicated the upper low \nresponsible for the active weather the last couple of days is\ncurrently centered over NM. Much of the area is currently on the \nsubsident side of the low with drier air building in. A few \nshowers, however, may still develop mainly across far eastern Gila\nCounty this afternoon along the deformation zone on the back side\nof the low. Otherwise, with the drier air and ridging aloft \ngradually building in from the eastern Pacific, dry conditions \nwith zero rain chances will prevail through the rest of the week \nunder mostly clear skies. \n\nDue to the lingering influences of the negative 500 mb height \nanomalies from the departing upper low, afternoon high temperatures \ntoday across much of the south-central AZ lower deserts will only \ntop out in the low 80s, which is a solid 10 degrees below normal for \nthis time of the year, while warmer readings in the 90s can be expected\nacross the western deserts. As the ridging aloft gradually builds\nover the region during the next couple of days, temperatures \nthrough the end of the week will gradually warm up with lower \ndesert highs returning to the mid 90s across south-central AZ to \naround 100 degrees along and west of the Lower Colorado River \nValley by Friday.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nModel guidance shows upper-lvl ridging migrating over the Four \nCorners region by this weekend with 500 mb hght fields increasing \nto around 588-590 dam. As this occurs, global models show a weak \nupper- low developing over N MX and far W TX which will induce \neasterly flow aloft over our forecast area. The increased pressure\ngradient between both features will bring breezy conditions to \nsoutheastern and southcentral AZ from Saturday through Monday. \nElevated winds will likely develop each morning and persist into \nthe early afternoon with gusts reaching 30-35 mph over the high \nterrain and 20- 25 mph across the lower deserts. This sfc easterly\nflow will help keep high temperatures near normal across \nsouthcentral AZ, but overnight lows will be elevated in the low to\nmid 70s. The 500 mb ridge axis looks to shift back to the over \nSoCal and the Great Basin early next week as a weak trough passes \nthrough the Intermountain West. This pattern should maintain \ntemperatures in a near normal range across central and eastern \nportions of the region, but allow highs to warm around 3-5 degrees\nabove normal across the western deserts where highs will likely \nreach the low 100s each afternoon.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1710Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\nNo major aviation concerns are expected during the TAF period.\nWinds will be very light with extended periods of VRB conditions.\nThe typical westerly shift is not anticipated this afternoon,\nthough some occasional observations are still likely. Cloud decks\nas low as 6-8 kft will linger through early this afternoon ahead\nof clear skies through the remainder of the forecast.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo aviation concerns are expected during the next 24 hours under\nclear skies. Outside of extended periods of VRB conditions,\nsoutherly/southeasterly flow will be common at bother terminals,\nwith speeds generally aob 10 kt.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nAside from a few afternoon showers across the far eastern \ndistricts today, dry conditions will prevail during the next \nseveral days. Temperatures will remain below normal across \nsouth-central AZ and near normal for areas along and west of the \nColorado River Valley through the rest of this week with gradual \nwarming anticipated through the weekend. Relative humidity will \nbottom out around 15-30% across the western districts and remain \nabove 40% across the eastern districts through the end of the week\nalong with good to excellent overnight recoveries. Winds will \nbe light and diurnal with speeds remaining predominantly 10 mph \nor less over the next few days before a stronger easterly gradient\nwind sets in by this weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Lojero\nLONG TERM...Salerno \nAVIATION...RW \nFIRE WEATHER...Lojero/Salerno\n",
